# stdio server documented in the CLI reference.
/mcp                /docs/cli#mcp       301
# `landing/get` is a FILE (the installer script), so /get/ is not a directory.
# A relative `get` link resolved against /get produced /get/get. The old
# index-file spelling under /get/ (fixed via urlmap.HREF_FIXUPS) keeps its 404:
# seo_lint rule 64 forbids `.html` sources here.
/get/get            /docs/install       301
# The recipes live under /docs/recipes/; a bare /recipes was linked from an
# older README translation.
/recipes            /docs/recipes/      301
# Punctuation glued onto a URL by a text extractor (a trailing `;` after
# llms.txt, a trailing backtick after /recipes or after llms.txt inside a
# Markdown code span). Each rule uses the spelling Pages matches, checked on a
# preview deploy: `;` stays literal, a backtick arrives as %60.
/llms.txt;          /llms.txt           301
/llms.txt%60        /llms.txt           301
/recipes%60         /docs/recipes/      301

# ── NO CATCH-ALL. Ever. ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There must never be a rule whose source matches every path — `/* ... 200`,
# `/* ... 301`, `/:slug ...`. Cloudflare Pages decides not-found handling from
# the published tree: with a top-level `404.html` an unmatched path gets that
# page and a real HTTP 404; a catch-all here takes the path first and the 404
# never happens. That is a soft 404 — every typo'd URL becomes a duplicate of
# whatever the rule points at, and a removed page can no longer be signalled
# as removed. Until 2026-08-22 this site had no `404.html` at all and Pages
# treated it as a single-page app: `/`, `/answers/what-is-xerj` and
# `/this-page-has-never-existed-xyz123` all returned 200 with the same
# 21,841-byte homepage body and the same ETag.
#
# `seo_lint.py` rule 67 fails the build on a catch-all source and on a missing
# `landing/404.html`. Named prefixes (`/docs/*`) are fine: they claim a real
# section instead of claiming everything.

